The method `merge` merges the sorted ranges `a[lo..mid]` and `a[mid + 1..hi]` into one sorted range `a[lo..hi]`.

```java
public static void sort(int[] a, int lo, int hi) {
    if (lo >= hi) {
        return;
    }
    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
    sort(a, lo, mid);
    sort(a, mid + 1, hi);
    merge(a, lo, mid, hi);
    String s = "";
    for (int i = lo; i <= hi; i++) {
        s += a[i];
    }
    System.out.print(s + " ");
}

public static void merge(int[] a, int lo, int mid, int hi) {
    int[] t = new int[hi - lo + 1];
    int i = lo, j = mid + 1, k = 0;
    while (i <= mid && j <= hi) {
        if (a[i] <= a[j]) {
            t[k++] = a[i++];
        } else {
            t[k++] = a[j++];
        }
    }
    while (i <= mid) {
        t[k++] = a[i++];
    }
    while (j <= hi) {
        t[k++] = a[j++];
    }
    for (k = 0; k < t.length; k++) {
        a[lo + k] = t[k];
    }
}
```

```java
int[] a = {5, 2, 4, 1, 3};
sort(a, 0, a.length - 1);
System.out.println();
```

